        "Attributes": [
          {
            "Id": 758313,
            "attributeType": "LongString",
            "attributeName": "AIDescription",
            "attributeRelationType": "None",
            "Value": "The Nintendo GameCube, released in 2001, marked Nintendo’s fourth foray into home consoles and a direct competitor to the PlayStation 2 and Xbox. Departing from cartridges, it utilized proprietary miniDVD optical discs, a design choice prioritizing developer ease and cost-effectiveness. Powered by a custom IBM \"Gekko\" CPU and an ArtX \"Flipper\" GPU, the GameCube delivered impressive performance for its time. Notably, it lacked DVD playback capabilities, focusing solely on gaming. \n\nThe console fostered a strong library of exclusive titles, including critically acclaimed games like *Super Smash Bros. Melee*, *Metroid Prime*, *The Legend of Zelda: The Wind Waker*, and *Resident Evil 4*. While commercially less successful than its rivals, the GameCube’s innovative controller and unique game experiences cemented its legacy, with its design influencing subsequent Nintendo consoles and its games remaining beloved classics."
          },
